  "summary": "Ghosts of the Asaro: clay-grey mud masks with errant fingers, born of a legendary retreat from raiders.",
  "description": "Asaro tradition says warriors fleeing a raid hid in a mud flat and emerged ghost-grey, terrifying their enemies into flight. The strategy hardened into the holosa masks — heavyweight clay over bamboo frames shaped with open mouths, pinched noses and long dangling clay fingers — worn bare-bodied, chalked grey, at sing-sing gatherings. Since the 1950s the Mudmen have become Papua New Guinea's most photographed performers, dancing a staged ghost-hunt that turns a war story into Highlands identity.",
